A football trip is even more rewarding when you fit in more than one match. In some regions the stadiums are so close together that you can see two, sometimes even three, matches in a single weekend. Below are the classics first, then an overview of more regions, and finally how to plan a trip like this sensibly.

## Manchester and Liverpool

Manchester and Liverpool are about 50 kilometres apart, and the train takes a little over an hour. In Manchester you have [Manchester City](/en/club/manchester-city/) and [Manchester United](/en/club/manchester-united/), in Liverpool [Liverpool](/en/club/liverpool/) and [Everton](/en/club/everton/). That is four clubs within an hour's travel, so a Saturday in one city and a Sunday in the other is quite doable. Keep in mind that many English clubs sell their tickets mainly to members, so you need to be early.

## The Ruhr area

In the Ruhr area, the cities and stadiums are close together and public transport is good. [Borussia Dortmund](/en/club/borussia-dortmund/) and [Schalke 04](/en/club/schalke-04/) are about 35 kilometres apart, and the train takes half an hour to three quarters of an hour. Bochum, Essen, Duisburg, Düsseldorf and Mönchengladbach are all within about an hour's travel too, and [1. FC Köln](/en/club/1-fc-koln/) and [Bayer Leverkusen](/en/club/bayer-leverkusen/) are on the edge of the area, about 20 kilometres apart. Which division each of these clubs plays in differs per season, so first check who is at home. Many German clubs let your match ticket double as a public transport ticket in the region, so check with the club.

## Northern Italy

There are more clubs around Milan than you might think. [Internazionale](/en/club/internazionale/) and [AC Milan](/en/club/ac-milan/) share San Siro and therefore never play at home at the same time, but you can combine them in the same trip with [Atalanta](/en/club/atalanta/) in Bergamo and [Como 1907](/en/club/como-1907/) on Lake Como. Both cities are about 50 kilometres from Milan and can be reached by train in roughly three quarters of an hour to an hour. [AC Monza](/en/club/ac-monza/) is close by too. A weekend with a match in Milan, one in Bergamo and an afternoon by the lake in between is one of the nicest football combinations in Europe.

## London

Which clubs play at home depends on the season and the division. London has many clubs, including [Arsenal](/en/club/arsenal/), [Chelsea](/en/club/chelsea/), [Tottenham Hotspur](/en/club/tottenham-hotspur/), West Ham, Fulham, Crystal Palace and [Brentford](/en/club/brentford/), plus clubs from the Championship. Everything is reachable by tube, train or bus, so you can see two matches in one weekend without leaving the city.

## Flanders

In Belgium, Club Brugge, Cercle Brugge, Gent, Anderlecht, Union SG, Antwerp and Mechelen are easy to combine by train. The distances between these cities are small, often around half an hour to an hour. Brussels is a logical base, because from there you can easily reach all the cities.

## Lisbon, Madrid, Prague and Vienna

These are real city weekends: the stadiums are in the city and can be reached by metro or tram. In Lisbon you have Benfica and Sporting, among others, plus smaller clubs such as Estoril and Casa Pia. In Madrid there are [Real Madrid](/en/club/real-madrid/), Atlético, Getafe, Rayo Vallecano and Leganés. Prague has Sparta, Slavia, Bohemians and Dukla, and in Vienna you find Rapid Wien and Austria Wien, plus smaller clubs.

## Istanbul

Galatasaray, Fenerbahçe and Beşiktaş are all in the same city, plus a number of smaller clubs. Fenerbahçe is on the Asian side and the other two on the European side, so expect to take a ferry or the metro between the stadiums. Check in advance how sales work there, because Turkish clubs often require a special supporter pass.

## Glasgow

Celtic and Rangers are in the same city, on either side of the River Clyde, and both are known for their atmosphere. Partick Thistle and Motherwell, among others, play nearby. If both clubs are at home in the same weekend, you can take in both.

## Copenhagen and Malmö

Copenhagen and Malmö are connected by the Øresund Bridge, and the train takes about half an hour between the two cities. FC København, Brøndby and Malmö FF therefore fit in one trip, across two countries.

## How to plan it

First choose the region, then look at which clubs play at home. Find a weekend where two of those clubs play at home on different days. On the home page you can filter by club, competition and period, so you can quickly see which dates overlap. On [This week](/en/news/) you can also see what goes on sale in the coming days.

Watch out for kick-off times: in many competitions they are only confirmed a few weeks ahead, because television moves them. So book flights and hotel with a cancellation option, or wait until both kick-off times are known. This is especially true for the Premier League and Serie A.

Also check when sales start for each match. Two clubs rarely put tickets on sale at the same time, and one of the two often requires a membership or club card. You can read more in our tips on [sale phases](/en/tips/sales-phases-explained/) and [buying tickets abroad](/en/tips/buying-tickets-abroad/).

Finally, pick a smart base. In northern Italy Milan is the logical choice, in the Ruhr area you can often stay in Dortmund or Essen, in Flanders in Brussels, and in England Manchester or Liverpool itself is a good base. That way you only check in once and travel by train on match day.
